Cache text advances, and stop a missing glyph hanging layout

Laying a page out measures text constantly: every string, every word and
every trial line break asks for widths, and each of those walked the
string a character at a time through the FreeType cache manager. On the
device a trivial local page spent 1.5 seconds in layout; the same page
takes no measurable time where the font backend is the internal bitmap
one, so the measuring is the cost, not the page.

The rendering is not what costs. FreeType keeps rendered glyphs, so a
repeated character is a cache hit; it is the lookup machinery itself,
run once per character, plus two calls into libparserutils to decode
each one. An advance never changes for a given face and size, so keep
the ones already looked up, and decode a byte below 0x80 without asking
anybody. Measured against DejaVu Sans through the real functions:
16 times quicker.

Widths do not move. The table is filled through the same lookup with the
same load flags, so hinting rounds every advance exactly as before, and
fb_font_width, fb_font_position and fb_font_split were checked against
the original algorithms over seven sizes, fourteen strings including
Cyrillic, CJK and punctuation, and eight break widths: identical offsets
and identical widths in every case. (Dropping FT_LOAD_RENDER instead
would have been faster to write and wrong: the autohinter changes the
advance, so text would have been measured to one width and drawn at
another.)

The same loops had a way to hang. In fb_font_position and fb_font_split
a character with no glyph did `continue', which skipped the only place
nxtchr is advanced -- the bottom of the loop -- so the loop ran forever
and the browser stopped inside layout with no way out. fb_font_width was
safe because it advances before the check. Both now step over the
character.

scripts/fonttest.sh builds the real functions against the netsurf
libraries and runs both checks.

